[{:name "a-check-step-is-a-task",
             :plan
             "What exists: bin/check's steps run as a script (`seon.check`), beside the reconciler. Correct: each step is a task in resources/seon/tasks/seon.check.edn carrying :seon.task/argv and :needs; the reconciler runs it as a child through one effect owner (`seon.fx`, an exclusive step running alone); its outcome is the run's end facts (exit, signal, output path); the run ends on the quiet fact and names the red step with what it blocks. One machinery for agents and checks; replaces the check script's own ordering. Shown in a prototype (e7_one_system): 9 steps in 1.46 s against 1.9 s serial, exclusive steps never overlapped. A first implementation on an older base is jolt/runner b4ea59d (reference only: :check tasks, an :fx/proc child owner, deftests a-red-check-step-blocks-only-its-dependents, a-check-step-passes-by-its-own-expected-exit, an-exclusive-child-runs-alone).",
             :kind :design,
             :needs
             #{"clojure.core.async.flow/a-graph-counts-its-messages-and-says-when-it-is-quiet"},
             :proofs
             #{seon.check-test/check-steps-run-by-needs-and-end-on-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "render-reads-the-reconcilers-index"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.render.proc/main-info` rebuilds from main's db, on every main commit, what the reconciler already keeps incrementally: `seon.reconcile/index`, `seon.task/tasks`, the runs, `seon.problems/report` and a datom count. Measured on a root of 92 tasks, 13 runs, 1,820 datoms: 113 ms per main commit (index 58 ms, tasks 26 ms, runs 8 ms, report 5 ms), against 0.87 ms for a quiet render pass. Correct: the reconciler emits each task row (state, blocked-by, proofs, runs, tokens), each run and the recorded problems as it changes them, and render reads those events; render's task rows, its index rebuild and its per-commit task and run reads go. Estimate: work per main commit in proportion to the tasks and runs that commit touched."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/render-rebuilds-no-index-per-commit
                      seon.render-test/render-main-commit-cost}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-flight-write-is-not-ignored"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every flight's write (:spawned, :started, :ran, :octopus-merged, then :done) goes through the writer, and the :done step of `seon.reconcile/advance` is `(done st)`: it drops the answer. Reproduction (jolt/runner e0efb69): the store hit MDB_MAP_FULL, the writer refused a run's end (`seon.writer/writer-one` answers :seon.writer/refused), the run stayed :running, the reconciler never reached quiet and nothing said why. Correct: an answer that is an error map is read: the flight records it where it can (the task's :seon.task/error by a smaller write, or the reconciler's state and ping-map as :refused with the run and message when main refuses every write), and quiescence counts the flight as ended with that error, so the run ends and names the error. The refused spawn (above) covers spawns only. A first fix on :check tasks is jolt/runner b880791 (WIP); its proof is re-targeted to an agent's run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-end-write-is-reported-at-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-octopus-merge-is-proven-by-one-combined-run"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: octopus merges (the reconciler's :octopus-merge op) and reach selection (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`), kept off by seon.reconcile/octopus-merges-off-as-decided until this exists. Correct: an octopus merge's members are proven together by one combined run of the tests their changes reach; on red the members are bisected (halves re-run) to name the one that broke it, and the green members merge. Replaces the off switch. Reference only: jolt/r5-port 302ce17 (parked, on an old base; the archived paper option3-R5-octopus (git history) merges.md); re-derive on main's reconciler."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-red-octopus-merge-is-bisected-to-its-member}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-design-is-done-when-its-proofs-are-designed-red"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` judges a :design run only by its plan changing (:no-design). Reproduction (agent-work audit): 4 of 4 designs wrote full bodies and no cost proof, and two passed as done while registering a 3-value status enum over 10 stored values. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §7, prototyped): design is done when a task needing it holds at least one proof and each such proof is designed red: every report :pass or :fail, or an :error whose ex-data carries :seon.error/type (a stub's :seon.error/unimplemented), with at least one not a pass. An untyped error (No such var) or an all-green proof is not designed red: error :not-designed-red naming the proof. Deletes: nothing; :no-design stays."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-design-is-refused-until-its-proofs-are-designed-red}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unreached-change-refuses-the-last-task"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` refuses red, unknown and stray tests, regressions, contract gaps and unmerged entries, but not a change no test reaches. v1's done rule refused 'functions no green test covers' at the last task of a chain, and its `my.test/check` answered :seon.test/unreached. Correct: at a task that carries :seon.task/target (the merge), every var in `seon.merge/divergence` against the branch's base that no green test's reach covers (the reach `seon.proofs/plan` selects by, over `seon.program/graph-index`) refuses {:error :uncovered :symbol s}; the context names each with the form that lists what reaches it. Deletes nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-unreached-change-refuses-at-the-merge}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-observed-retraction-leaves-no-residue"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session context-render-observes-what-changed (2026-10-24): `seon.reconcile/observe` of a retraction answers an index unequal to `seon.reconcile/index` of the same db. Retracting task demo/d leaves `:acc`, `:depth` and `:done` entries for demo/d and empty `:needed-by` sets for its needs (`put-task`, `derive-needs` and `refresh-done` move entries but never dissoc a gone one). `:tasks`, `:claims` and the ready set are right, so actions and the done rule read the same answers; the residue grows with retractions. Reproduction: seon.reconcile-test/an-index-observed-from-the-last-is-the-index-listed with the step [[:db/retractEntity [:seon.task/id \"demo/d\"]]] after its new boot. Correct: a gone entity's entries are dissoc'd where they are moved (the owner of each map), so an observed index equals the listed one. Proof (to write, red today): that retraction observed equals the index listed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-observed-retraction-leaves-no-residue}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-work-is-tasks"
  :seon.task/plan "PLANNED, NOT BUILT: waits for root's go and for the agent loop's park and send parts (design `docs/turn.md` on jolt/agent-loop, build parts 3-4). Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md: root is main; root's special cases go): root's own work is tasks run by agents on main's current commit, never a human-only sink.

What exists. A failure is `seon.reconcile/hold-tx`: the task `:held` with `:seon.task/failure {:reason :run}` and `:seon.task/evidence` (`seon.reconcile/evidence`: conflicts, unmerged, reds, problem), the run `:closed`. Its causes are `seon.reconcile/hold-tx`'s callers: `seon.reconcile/actions`' `failed` (the done rule refused, or the agent died) and the `:octopus-merged` step (a merge refused other than `:behind`). Nobody owns a held task: `seon.reconcile/root-causes` lists them, and `quiet` writes that list into ONE fact, `:seon.root/quiescent` on the root entity, which every later quiet spell overwrites, so a cause nobody read before the next spell is gone. Two more root failures are not even holds: a health problem a merge caused on main (`seon.problems/caused`, judged per branch before its merge, never on the octopus's combined result), and a failed build (`:seon.root/generation` `{:error}`, again one overwritten fact). Templates exist only for work kinds (`seon.template/instructions`: merge, design, implement, test).

Which failures become tasks, given the agent loop. Under the loop design a `:waiting`, `:cap`, `:no-progress` or provider-`:error` end parks with no hold, and a refused done-rule end holds and parks with its reason in the user's timeline (the loop's owner question 2): both stay the session's own, not root's. Root's are the five the owner named: a refused merge, a dead agent, health a merge caused, red proofs on main, a failed update.

The change (one mechanism, `seon.template`'s request path reused).
1. Five templates in `seon.template/instructions`, kinds `:root-merge :agent-died :health :red-proofs :update`, task namespace `root`, model and budgets from config like any task (`seon.reconcile/desired-env`): no kind switch in the reconciler; the agent's instructions say what to read and what ends it.
2. One new function in seon.template, root-task, from a failure (kind, the evidence map, the failed task and run, or main's t for health/red/update) to one task declaration. Its id is the cause's own: `root/merge-<run>`, `root/died-<run>`, `root/health-<main t>`, `root/red-<test>@<digest>`, `root/update-<commit>`. So the same failure seen twice is the same task (an upsert of identical facts) and two failures are two tasks: nothing overwrites.
3. Each producer writes the task in the SAME transaction as the fact of the failure: `hold-tx` (merge refused, agent died) gains the task's datoms; `seon.prove`'s result write for main gains a `:red-proofs` task when a test turned red at a digest that was green; the octopus merge's `:committed` write gains a `:health` task when `seon.problems/caused` of main after vs before is non-empty (cost to measure first: the per-branch `:caused` already runs in each judge); `my.root/build!`'s generation write gains an `:update` task on `{:error}`. One write per failure, as `request-tx` writes a linked set.
4. The task runs like any task: the reconciler spawns its agent forked from main's committed head (main's current program: the fork server holds it, `program/load-changes!`), on its own branch `root/<id>` (its writes, if any, merge by its own merge task, `with-merge`, through the one merge path). A root task's agent never holds another session's branch.
5. How each relates to the failed task (the parked agent of the loop design is never killed by it):
   - `:root-merge`: the failed merge task is held and its agent parked on its branch. The root task's agent reads the evidence and the branch (read-only: `my.branch` reads of another branch), then acts through the one send: a message to that session (wakes it, with the conflicts and what to do) or `my.task/fail!` of the original with a reason. Messaging a held task from root releases it in the same transaction (loop design section 3). The root task ends done when it has sent its decision; the original's own next run is judged as any run.
   - `:agent-died`: the run is `:dead` and the session has no process (loop design: the next message resumes it at its head). The root task reads exit status, stderr tail and `last-op`; it resumes the session by a message, or files a platform task (a defect in what died), or fails the original.
   - `:health`, `:red-proofs`: work tasks on main's current commit: make the problem entries or the reds go away, proved by `seon.problems/caused` empty and the reds green; their fix merges by the merge task like any work.
   - How a `:health` task closes when its cause is gone: no new rule; its proof is the problem entry it names reading `:open? false` or being absent (`seon.problems/closed?`: a fact written on the error's subject after its last occurrence). So an entry a live proc makes untrue closes the task without an agent's fix: the reconciler's observe sees the proof true and the task done, and the spawn it would have made is not made. That needs every recorded error to name a subject whose later write means its cause is gone. Today a `:platform` error has none: a past boot's `:seon.watch/died` stays open while this boot's watcher is alive (task `seon.problems/a-platform-error-closes-when-its-cause-is-gone`), which merges first. A health task for an entry with no such subject is the agent's to judge: it reads the cause, and ends the task either with a fix or with `my.task/fail!` and its reason.
   - `:update`: the build's error is its evidence; fix on main as work, or the build input that failed.
6. No recursion: a root task's own failure (its merge refused, its agent dead) is held and parked for the user, with no root task of a root task (the template reads the failed task's ns: `root` gets none). That is the one human sink left, at the top.
7. Deleted: the `:root-causes` member of `:seon.root/quiescent` and its write in `quiet` (the quiet spell keeps its `{:t}` fact: the signal tests and `seon.await` wait on, an event, not a store of causes); `seon.reconcile/root-causes` stays only as the render of root's page (the open `root/*` tasks and the held tasks that block released work), reading tasks, storing nothing; the `{:error}` overwrite of `:seon.root/generation` stays as the build's answer, the task is its record.

Correctness: every failure is a durable task with an owner, a run, proofs and an end; two failures keep two causes; nothing a later spell writes erases one. Perf: one write per failure (the task's datoms ride the failure's own transaction); the health check of a merge is its one added cost, to measure (estimate: one `seon.problems/caused` over main before and after the octopus, which the judge already does per branch). Lines: about +120 (templates, root-task, producers) and -40 (root-causes storage, quiescent's causes): a capability, net up, complexity down.

Proofs (to write red first): two failures one after the other (a refused merge, then a dead agent, with a quiet spell between and after) keep both causes, each as a `root/*` task, after every later quiet spell; the same failure observed twice is one task; a red proof on main makes one `:red-proofs` task naming the test and digest; a root task's own failure makes no root task; a root task's agent forks from main's head at its spawn (its program digest equals main's), on its own branch.

Questions for root: (a) admission: do root tasks take places under `:seon.config.agent/max-running` like any task, or go first (a config key, the owner's gate)? Recommended: like any task, ordered first in `actions`' starts. (b) does a `:root-merge` message the parked session (recommended: the session has the context) or work on its branch itself (needs the branch free)? (c) health on main per merge (cost measured first) or only when bin/prove's health step reds on main (cheaper, later)?"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/two-failures-in-a-row-keep-both-causes-as-tasks
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-failure-seen-twice-is-one-task
                      seon.prove-test/a-red-proof-on-main-is-a-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-root-tasks-failure-makes-no-root-task
                      seon.agent-test/a-root-task-forks-from-mains-head
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-health-task-closes-when-its-row-closes}}]
